[0.001, "o", "I counted the crashes in the SWITRS collisions table by county, using one row per crash, for the whole period the data covers. The  \r\nchecked answer is below.                                                                                                            \r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "Los Angeles County has the most reported collisions. From 2001-01-01 to 2021-06-03: Los Angeles had 2,851,925 crashes, Los Angeles  \r\nshare 30.26% of all crashes. Orange is a distant second: Orange had 728,565, Orange share 7.73%. Next come San Bernardino 569,376,  \r\nSan Diego 535,596 and Riverside 493,758. These are raw counts, not per-capita rates.                                                \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCaveats\u001b[0m                                                                                                                             \r\n\r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThe data covers 2001-01-01 to 2021-06-03.                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mOnly crashes reported to police are in the data.                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThese are raw counts. The data has no population figures, so it cannot rank places per capita.                                   \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mEvidence\u001b[0m                          "]
