[0.008, "o", "Riverside has the highest fatal share of the top five: Riverside 5,065 fatal crashes, Riverside 1.026% of its collisions. Next: San \r\nBernardino 0.981%, San Diego 0.9%, Orange 0.47%, Los Angeles 0.465%. Los Angeles has the most fatal crashes in absolute terms (Los  \r\nAngeles 13,265) but the lowest share, because it has so many non-fatal crashes. This is a descriptive comparison; it does not show  \r\nwhy the shares differ (road type, speeds, reporting of minor crashes and other factors may all contribute).                         \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCaveats\u001b[0m                                                                                                                             \r\n\r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThe data covers 2001-01-01 to 2021-06-03.                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mOnly crashes reported to police are in the data.                                                                                 \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mThese are raw counts. The data has no pop"]
